{ignore}
<p><code>{recurse}</code> is where the children of the current row go. It renders the body of
the enclosing <a href="?tag/tree">{tree}</a> - the content as written between
<code>{tree}</code> and <code>{/tree}</code> - again, once for every child, each child a row of
its own with its own fields. Inside that body <code>{recurse}</code> works again one level
down, so the tree is rendered to any depth. A row without children renders nothing here.</p>

<p>The new list is a level named as the tree is: <code>depth@tree</code> is one more than the
level above, and <code>first@tree</code>, <code>current@tree</code> and <code>count@tree</code>
speak of the children being rendered. The tree's <code>sort</code>, <code>where</code> and
<code>reverse</code> options are carried along, so children are ordered and filtered as their
parents were.</p>

<p>Usually it stands inside <a href="?tag/branch">{branch}</a>, so the list markup around it
only appears for a row that has children. The tag takes no parameters; under the strict syntax
check a <code>{recurse}</code> outside a tree is an error.</p>
{/ignore}
